<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thuan Hoa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Thuận Hóa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thuan Hoa was the name of a frontier region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, roughly the modern provinces of Quang Tri and Thua Thien Hue, formed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> territory ceded to Dai Viet in 1306. In 1558 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-hoang">Nguyen Hoang</a> became its governor, and it became the base from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a> ruled southern Vietnam, with their capital eventually at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hue">Phu Xuan</a> (Hue).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thuan-hoa.jpg" alt="Thuan Hoa" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Doãn Hiệu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VietNam(1226-1400).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
